menu "Flight Computer Task Priorities"

comment "Higher number = higher priority. FreeRTOS runs the highest-"
comment "priority READY task; equal priorities round-robin."

# =====================================================================
#  Why these live together in ONE file
#
#  Task priorities are only meaningful relative to each other. Splitting
#  them across per-component Kconfig files would let you set two tasks
#  to the same number, or invert an intended ordering, without ever
#  seeing both values on screen at once. Keeping them in a single
#  menuconfig page makes the whole ordering reviewable at a glance.
#
#  These are Kconfig.projbuild options, so CONFIG_FC_PRIO_* is visible
#  to every component, not just main.
# =====================================================================

config FC_PRIO_DISCRETE
    int "External discrete input handler tasks"
    range 1 24
    default 11
    help
        Tasks woken by the Blue Raven discrete GPIO interrupts -- the
        staging line (J8) and the recovery line (J7). Both tasks share
        this priority. Each BR output is split two ways: one branch to
        actuation hardware, one to this board, which only listens.

        NOTE: this is the TASK priority, not the interrupt priority.
        The ISR itself preempts every task on this list regardless of
        what any of these numbers say -- see FC_DISCRETE_ISR_LEVEL below
        if you need to change the interrupt's own level.

        Highest task priority in the system: a staging or recovery
        event is time-critical and must not wait behind a sensor read.

config FC_PRIO_SENSORS
    int "Sensor reader tasks (BMP388, LSM6DSOX, GPS)"
    range 1 24
    default 9
    help
        Shared by all three sensor reader tasks. They sleep on an
        interrupt notification and do a short SPI/UART read on wake, so
        they need to run promptly but hold the CPU only briefly.

        All three share one priority deliberately: none is more urgent
        than the others, and equal priorities round-robin, which spreads
        CPU fairly when two fire close together.

config FC_PRIO_DEPLOY
    int "Deployment logic task"
    range 1 24
    default 7
    help
        Consumes sensor data and decides on deployment events.

        Below the sensors on purpose: it can only act on data the
        sensors have already produced, so starving a sensor to run this
        sooner would just make it reason about staler numbers.

config FC_PRIO_TELEMETRY
    int "Telemetry / XBee task"
    range 1 24
    default 5
    help
        Transmits downlink frames over the XBee.

        Above the SD writer because telemetry is the backup record: if
        the card is lost or corrupted, whatever got transmitted is all
        that survives. A radio frame also has a hard deadline that a
        disk write does not -- miss the slot and the data is simply
        gone, whereas a delayed SD write still lands.

config FC_PRIO_SD_WRITER
    int "SD card writer task"
    range 1 24
    default 3
    help
        Drains the log queue and writes to the card.

        Lowest of the five despite SD being the primary storage. This
        is not a statement about importance -- it is about latency
        tolerance. SD writes stall unpredictably (block erase, wear
        levelling) for tens of milliseconds, and a stall at a higher
        priority would block genuinely time-critical work. The queue in
        front of this task is what makes the low priority safe: samples
        buffer up rather than being lost while the card is busy.

        If queue_full_drops in sd_logger_diag_t starts climbing, raise
        SD_LOGGER_QUEUE_DEPTH before raising this priority.

config FC_PRIO_BATTERY
    int "Battery monitor task"
    range 1 24
    default 2
    help
        Samples the pack voltage divider on ADC1 and publishes it as
        its own log stream.

        Below the SD writer because it is the only task on this list
        whose output nothing else waits on -- a battery sample arriving
        a few milliseconds late costs nothing, while delaying the writer
        costs queue headroom for every other stream.

        Above print because it produces flight record and print
        produces console noise.

endmenu

menu "Flight Computer Interrupt Levels"

config FC_DISCRETE_ISR_LEVEL
    int "External discrete GPIO interrupt level (1-3)"
    range 1 3
    default 1
    help
        Hardware interrupt level for the external discrete GPIOs, NOT a
        task priority -- a different scale entirely.

        Levels 1-3 can be written in C. Levels 4+ require hand-written
        assembly and cannot call any FreeRTOS API, so this is capped
        at 3 on purpose.

        Level 1 is correct for almost everything. Raise it only if you
        measure a discrete edge being serviced too late, and know that
        doing so lets it preempt other level-1 ISRs including the
        sensor DRDY handlers.

endmenu

menu "Flight Computer SD Card Policy"

config FC_SD_AUTO_EJECT_S
    int "Unmount the SD card after this many seconds of uptime (0 = never)"
    range 0 604800
    default 21600
    help
        A vehicle left powered on the pad otherwise sits with a mounted
        card until somebody sends EJECT SD or pulls the battery, and
        pulling the battery on a mounted FAT filesystem is exactly what
        the eject path exists to avoid.

        The default is 21600 (6 hours), comfortably longer than a range
        day and far shorter than leaving it powered overnight.

        The deadline is DEFERRED, never enforced, while the vehicle is
        ARMED or in FLIGHT -- the same gate the uplink EJECT SD command
        uses. A pad hold that runs long and then launches cannot lose
        the flight to this timer; the unmount waits until the vehicle is
        back in STANDBY or LANDED.

        Set 0 to disable the timer and keep the card mounted until an
        uplink eject or power down.

        NOTE: tools/ground_station.py shows a countdown derived from
        this value and keeps its own copy of it. Change one and change
        the other, or the countdown will be wrong.

endmenu

menu "Flight Computer Optional Subsystems"

config FC_ENABLE_DISCRETE_IN
    bool "Watch the Blue Raven staging / recovery discrete inputs"
    default y
    help
        ON for flight. This is the board's independent record of when
        the Blue Raven actually fired -- the whole reason the J7/J8
        dividers exist, and half of the point of laying this vehicle's
        timeline next to the Blue Raven's afterwards.

        Turning it OFF is a BENCH convenience, for working on telemetry
        or flight logic without two floating inputs generating thousands
        of spurious edges a second. Nothing else depends on these
        handles -- discrete_in_fired() is never called and flight_logic
        never reads them, so they are pure observers and disabling them
        cannot change what any other subsystem does.

        Try DISCRETE_IN_INTERNAL_PULLDOWN first. That keeps the feature
        and fixes the same bench problem, and is the option you want
        unless you specifically need these two tasks gone.

        A build with this OFF logs a loud warning on every boot, because
        a flight computer that silently stopped watching its deployment
        signals is exactly the kind of thing that gets discovered after
        the flight.

endmenu

menu "Flight Computer Operating Mode"

choice FC_OPERATING_MODE
    prompt "Operating mode"
    default FC_MODE_FLIGHT
    help
        FLIGHT is the real thing and the default. Every other option on
        this page only exists when TEST is selected.

config FC_MODE_FLIGHT
    bool "Flight"
    help
        Normal behaviour. Boots in STANDBY; the mode changes only on an
        uplink ARM/DISARM or a detected liftoff/landing.

config FC_MODE_TEST
    bool "Test: Blue Raven staging/recovery signals only -- NOT FOR FLIGHT"
    help
        Bench test of the two Blue Raven discrete lines and nothing else.

        STARTED: the staging (J8, GPIO22) and recovery (J7, GPIO23)
        inputs, with exactly the settings under Component config ->
        Discrete inputs (mode, resistors, thresholds); the SD card, so
        each firing lands in event.bin as it would in flight; and a
        console printout of both lines every FC_TEST_PRINT_MS.

        NOT STARTED: IMU, barometer, sensor bus, XBee radio, GPS, battery
        monitor, flight logic, status records. None of them needs to be
        connected, and nothing is transmitted -- watch the USB console.

        Each line prints its voltage, its running idle average, its peak,
        whether it is FIRED now, and how many firings were accepted.
        Fire the Blue Raven's outputs (its own test function, or a
        bench supply on the line) and watch them change.

        Overrides FC_ENABLE_DISCRETE_IN: the lines are always watched in
        this mode, since watching them is the whole point.

endchoice

config FC_TEST_PRINT_MS
    int "Console printout period in the Blue Raven test (ms)"
    depends on FC_MODE_TEST
    range 100 10000
    default 500
    help
        How often both lines' readings are printed. Firings are logged
        the instant they are detected regardless of this.

endmenu

menu "Flight Computer Power Saving"

# =====================================================================
#  Standby current. The board was measured at ~120 mA on the 3V3 rail
#  in STANDBY and ~200 mA in flight (2026-10-04). Everything in this
#  menu changes what the board draws on the pad and nothing about how
#  it behaves once ARMED: every option here either never touches a
#  flight path, or is explicitly undone the moment the mode goes fast.
# =====================================================================

config FC_PHY_HOLD_RESET
    bool "Hold the module's Ethernet PHY in reset"
    default y
    help
        The ESP32-P4-ETH carries an IP101 Ethernet PHY that this firmware
        never uses. Out of reset it powers its analog front end and keeps
        sending link pulses to a cable that is not there, with no code
        asking it to. Driving its RST_N low at the top of app_main stops
        that for the life of the boot.

        Turn this off only if a build ever needs Ethernet.

config FC_PHY_RESET_GPIO
    int "PHY reset GPIO (IP101 RST_N, active low)"
    depends on FC_PHY_HOLD_RESET
    range 0 54
    default 51
    help
        GPIO51 on the Waveshare ESP32-P4-ETH. Internal to the module --
        not on the J1 header, so it collides with nothing on PCB v1.

config FC_GPS_OFF_IN_STANDBY
    bool "Power the GPS down in STANDBY"
    default y
    help
        Drives the PA1616D's EN (GPIO33) low while the vehicle is in
        STANDBY and high in ARMED, FLIGHT and LANDED. Saves roughly the
        module's tracking current (~20 mA on the 3V3 rail).

        Cost: fix delay after ARM. With the breakout's CR1220 coin cell
        fitted the module keeps time and satellite data and is back in
        seconds; without it, every ARM is a cold start of 30 s or more.
        An IMU-triggered launch from STANDBY flies its first seconds with
        no fix. STATUS_FLAG_GPS_FIX reads clear while the module is off.

config FC_PM_DFS
    bool "Scale the CPU clock down in STANDBY"
    depends on PM_ENABLE
    default y
    help
        ESP-IDF dynamic frequency scaling: when nothing holds a power-
        management lock the CPU drops to FC_PM_MIN_FREQ_MHZ, and drivers
        raise it on their own for the duration of each transaction.

        Outside STANDBY the firmware holds an ESP_PM_CPU_FREQ_MAX lock,
        so ARMED, FLIGHT and LANDED run at full clock exactly as before.
        The lock is taken from inside the mode transition itself, so an
        IMU liftoff out of STANDBY is at full clock in the same instant.

        Requires Component config -> Power Management -> Support for
        power management (CONFIG_PM_ENABLE).

config FC_PM_MIN_FREQ_MHZ
    int "Minimum CPU frequency in STANDBY (MHz)"
    depends on FC_PM_DFS
    default 40
    help
        40 is the crystal frequency, the lowest DFS will go. Standby
        work is a 12.5 Hz IMU read, a 1.5 Hz baro read and a 1 Hz status
        record -- microseconds each even at 40 MHz.

endmenu

menu "Flight Computer Battery Sense"

# =====================================================================
#  How the pack voltage on GPIO20 (ADC1_CH4) is turned back into a
#  battery voltage:
#
#      battery_v = adc_v * (R_top + R_bottom) / R_bottom * gain + offset
#
#  The resistor values set the divider; gain and offset trim what is
#  left after it, measured against a multimeter. Kconfig has no float
#  type, so everything here is an integer in ohms, millivolts or parts
#  per million. adc_v is logged untrimmed in batt.bin, so a calibration
#  done later can still be applied to an old log.
# =====================================================================

config FC_VBAT_R_TOP_OHMS
    int "Divider top resistor: battery+ to the sense node (ohms)"
    range 1 10000000
    default 6800
    help
        The resistor from BT1+ to the divider node (R1 on PCB v1).

        Watch the ratio: the pin must stay below ~3.1 V at full charge.
        For a 2S pack (8.4 V) that needs R_bottom / (R_top + R_bottom)
        below ~0.37. 6.8k / 3k is 0.306: 2.57 V on the pin at 8.4 V,
        and readable up to ~10.1 V. Two equal resistors (0.5) would put
        4.2 V on the pin, clipping the ADC and exceeding the chip's
        3.3 V supply. The boot log says so in red if the ratio is too
        large for FC_VBAT_PACK_MAX_MV.

        The divider draws pack / (R_top + R_bottom) continuously:
        ~0.76 mA for 6.8k + 3k on 7.4 V.

config FC_VBAT_R_BOTTOM_OHMS
    int "Divider bottom resistor: sense node to ground (ohms)"
    range 1 10000000
    default 3000
    help
        The resistor from the divider node to GND (R2 on PCB v1; the
        board's original design value was 2.7k, the fitted part is 3k). The series resistor into the pin (R3)
        carries no steady current and does not enter the ratio.

config FC_VBAT_GAIN_PPM
    int "Calibration gain (parts per million, 1000000 = x1.0)"
    range 500000 1500000
    default 1000000
    help
        Multiplies the reconstructed pack voltage. Corrects resistor
        tolerance and ADC gain error together.

        One-point calibration, offset left at 0:
          1. Measure the pack with a multimeter at the battery terminals.
          2. Read battery_v from the ground station or the console.
          3. Set this to 1000000 * meter / reported.
        Example: meter 7.92 V, firmware says 7.80 V -> 1015385.

        For two points (a full and a part-discharged pack), solve
        meter = reported * gain + offset for both.

config FC_VBAT_OFFSET_MV
    int "Calibration offset (mV, added after the gain)"
    range -2000 2000
    default 0
    help
        Added to the pack voltage after the gain. Leave at 0 unless a
        two-point calibration says otherwise.

config FC_VBAT_PACK_MAX_MV
    int "Highest pack voltage to expect (mV)"
    range 1000 30000
    default 8400
    help
        Full-charge voltage of the pack: 8400 for 2S LiPo, 12600 for 3S.
        Used only by a boot-time check that the divider above keeps the
        ADC pin inside its range at this voltage. It changes no reading.

endmenu

menu "Flight Computer Sensor Failover"

# =====================================================================
#  What flight_logic does when a sensor stops producing samples.
#  "Stops" is judged from the sample sequence number, so it covers an
#  unplugged chip, a reset one and a dead task alike. See flight_logic.h.
#
#    IMU silent      -> logic keeps running on altitude; tilt invalid for
#                       the rest of the flight if after liftoff; liftoff
#                       from altitude if before
#    barometer silent -> altitude, velocity and apogee from GPS
#    both             -> GPS altitude, IMU rules as above
# =====================================================================

config FC_FAILOVER_MS
    int "Sensor silent this long before switching to the backup (ms)"
    range 200 30000
    default 2000
    help
        How long the IMU or the barometer may produce no new sample
        before flight_logic stops waiting for it and uses the backup.

        Short on purpose. Apogee comes roughly 15-20 s after liftoff, and
        every millisecond of this is time the logic spends with stale
        altitude. Transient glitches are already absorbed below this
        level: a sensor driver only stops publishing after a failed ID
        check, and retries every 250 ms.

        In STANDBY the effective value is at least 5000 ms, because the
        barometer samples at 1.5 Hz there and would otherwise flap.

config FC_ALT_LIFTOFF_AGL_M
    int "Liftoff from altitude, when the IMU is lost (m above the pad)"
    range 5 500
    default 30
    help
        With the IMU silent, the accelerometer cannot detect liftoff, and
        nothing is evaluated before liftoff. Instead, liftoff is declared
        when altitude (barometer, or GPS if that is the live source) is
        above this for 3 consecutive barometer samples or 2 GPS fixes.

        It is late by however long the climb takes -- typically a
        second or two -- and TVAL timings shift by that much. It also
        moves the flight mode to FLIGHT, since with no IMU there is no
        wake-up interrupt to do it. Active only while the IMU is silent.

config FC_GPS_STALE_MS
    int "GPS fix older than this is not used for altitude (ms)"
    range 1500 30000
    default 3000
    help
        The GPS updates once a second. A fix older than this is treated
        as no fix, and with the barometer also silent the altitude
        source becomes NONE: every AGL/VEL/BVEL/PINC event reads false.

endmenu
